WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.260
Practical sanitizer use is built from durable habits.

2
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:13.640
Enable ASan and UBSan early enough that developers see memory-access and undefined-behavior defects while changes are still understandable.

3
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:19.740
Keep the build configuration visible, repeatable, and separate from ordinary production release builds.

4
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.380
Run tests that exercise meaningful paths.

5
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:33.240
Sanitizers can only report behavior they observe, so unit tests, integration tests, boundary tests, and targeted longer-running jobs all have a role.

6
00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.100
Read reports for root cause.

7
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:44.400
The first failing line may show the symptom, but the durable fix often involves ownership, lifetime, bounds, initialization, or numeric assumptions elsewhere.

8
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:48.120
Fix the design issue rather than merely quieting the report.

9
00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:55.020
Prefer RAII, containers, bounded views, deliberate initialization, and explicit contracts.

10
00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.760
Add regression tests around corrected behavior.

11
00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:06.180
Treat sanitizer findings as engineering defects and keep suppressions rare, specific, documented, and reviewed.

12
00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:11.340
Finally, place sanitizers inside a broader C++ safety program.

13
00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:17.920
They support review, static analysis, safer abstractions, modernization, and CI governance.

14
00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:24.900
Used this way, ASan and UBSan help teams find defects earlier, fix them more confidently, and maintain trust in the codebase over time.

15
00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:28.380
The lasting habit is consistency.

16
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:34.320
Keep sanitizer jobs reproducible, reports actionable, and ownership of fixes clear.

17
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:42.980
When developers trust the signal, sanitizer findings become part of normal engineering practice rather than an occasional emergency cleanup effort.
